±«˝˚N™Ç.^3C¶LíŸfVÖ“≥á7–]<≥úÑq¶ãÄ∆«ìÄëÕã;˛q/)∆#/Í˚.YfÀ5 ö6⁄ñâ®Øg0hÄÇ5›)ï5'≈FÍ\˙∫-á3ıÌ|›A5[ÉBc5Çºµ†=D9„¨˜˝9è“␀␀␀ˇˇB$lÅç,ßÑñZ␀␀␀ˇˇ␀)Ñ@–<␀,7:headers;1681:40:4:Date,29:Mon, 06 Apr 2026 08:56:06 GMT,]52:12:Content-Type,32:text/event-stream; charset=utf-8,]31:17:Transfer-Encoding,7:chunked,]28:10:Connection,10:keep-alive,]28:13:Cache-Control,8:no-cache,]52:38:anthropic-ratelimit-input-tokens-limit,7:2000000,]56:42:anthropic-ratelimit-input-tokens-remaining,7:1992000,]66:38:anthropic-ratelimit-input-tokens-reset,20:2026-04-06T08:56:05Z,]52:39:anthropic-ratelimit-output-tokens-limit,6:400000,]56:43:anthropic-ratelimit-output-tokens-remaining,6:400000,]67:39:anthropic-ratelimit-output-tokens-reset,20:2026-04-06T08:56:05Z,]45:34:anthropic-ratelimit-requests-limit,4:4000,]49:38:anthropic-ratelimit-requests-remaining,4:3999,]62:34:anthropic-ratelimit-requests-reset,20:2026-04-06T08:56:05Z,]46:32:anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-limit,7:2400000,]50:36:anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-remaining,7:2392000,]60:32:anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-reset,20:2026-04-06T08:56:05Z,]46:10:request-id,28:req_011CZnEn9qJtfswFD6k6C4qf,]77:25:strict-transport-security,44:max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload,]69:25:anthropic-organization-id,36:19d39eaa-efb4-49e0-afe8-a7359703719a,]23:6:Server,10:cloudflare,]40:29:x-envoy-upstream-service-time,4:1225,]27:16:Content-Encoding,4:gzip,]26:4:vary,15:Accept-Encoding,]46:13:server-timing,25:x-originResponse;dur=1227,]29:15:cf-cache-status,7:DYNAMIC,]208:10:set-cookie,189:_cfuvid=5rCeEpUtJFzKzkBR28E6jcd7IicVV4LUGDOzmF9P.nk-1775465764.8614576-1.0.1.1-DgBqxWsbWi9bSfXh_.J4Qf9dNc2AdPSZq85l42Z9DaM; HttpOnly; SameSite=None; Secure; Path=/; Domain=api.anthropic.com,]73:23:Content-Security-Policy,42:default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none',]23:12:X-Robots-Tag,4:none,]33:6:CF-RAY,20:9e7f93c66d84ade6-DUB,]]12:http_version;8:HTTP/1.1,}7:request;101561:4:path;22:/v1/messages?beta=true,9:authority;0:,6:scheme;5:https,6:method;4:POST,4:port;3:443#4:host;17:api.anthropic.com;13:timestamp_end;18:1775465764.7823298^15:timestamp_start;18:1775465764.7814496^8:trailers;0:~7:content;100293:{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# currentDate\nToday's date is 2026-04-06.\n\n      IMPORTANT: this context may or may not be relevant to your tasks. You should not respond to this context unless it is highly relevant to your task.\n</system-reminder>\n\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Design a complete implementation plan for a disk-backed LRU file cache Swift package for iOS. The workspace is empty ‚Äî this is a greenfield Swift Package.\n\n## Requirements Summary\n\n**Core features:**\n- Disk-backed LRU cache, multiple independent named caches, each with own config\n- Fast start: init returns immediately, no blocking I/O; operations are async/await\n- LRU order by last-accessed date; eviction is async, non-blocking\n- Eviction moves files to `tmp/` (not delete); handle filename collisions in tmp\n- Atomic writes for all insertions\n- Index (LRU metadata) persisted to disk, survives restarts\n\n**Configuration per cache:** name, max entry count, max total bytes, eviction debounce interval, iOS data protection class\n\n**API:**\n- `add(key, data)` ‚Äî insert or replace\n- `update(key, data)` ‚Äî alias for add\n- `modify(key, data)` ‚Äî replace existing; throw `CacheError.keyNotFound` if absent\n- `get(key) -> Data?` ‚Äî return data + bump LRU; nil if absent\n- `promote(key)` ‚Äî bump LRU without reading; throw `CacheError.keyNotFound` if absent\n\n**Constraints:**\n- Index persistence on `get`/`promote` must be debounced (avoid write amplification)\n- Index persistence on mutations (`add`, `modify`, eviction) must be immediate\n- Cache dir deleted externally ‚Üí recreate on next write\n- Corrupted index on init ‚Üí degrade gracefully to empty cache (no crash)\n- File in index but missing on disk at `get` time ‚Üí return nil, self-heal index immediately\n\n## Design considerations to address:\n\n1. **Concurrency model**: Use Swift actor for the cache. `init` kicks off async index load via `Task { await self.loadIndex() }`. Store this as `indexLoadTask: Task<Void, Never>`. Each public method begins with `await indexLoadTask?.value` so callers block only on their first call if index hasn't loaded yet, not on init. This is non-blocking from init perspective but naturally serializes.\n\n2. **File naming**: Use SHA256 hash of the key as filename in cache directory (deterministic, no need to store path separately). When moving to tmp, check for collision and append `-<UUID>` suffix if needed.\n\n3. **In-memory index structure**: `[String: CacheEntry]` dict for O(1) lookup. Running `totalBytes: Int` for size tracking. Eviction sorts by `lastAccessedDate` (O(n log n), acceptable since eviction is infrequent).\n\n4. **Debounce pattern**: Use a stored `Task` that gets cancelled and recreated on each debounce trigger. Immediate writes cancel any pending debounced write and write synchronously.\n\n5. **Atomic writes**: Use `Data.write(to:options:)` with `.atomic` option (write-to-temp + rename internally).\n\n6. **Data protection**: Apply `FileAttributeKey.protectionKey` when creating files and directory.\n\nPlease produce:\n- Package directory structure\n- File-by-file content design (types, method signatures, key implementation logic in pseudocode)\n- Test coverage plan\n- Any edge cases or tricky parts to call out\n\nBe detailed enough to implement from directly."}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll explore the workspace to understand the environment, then design the complete implementation plan."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LapdPXeMHXABZe8C17JHLG","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"ls /workspace && swift --version 2>/dev/null || echo \"swift not in PATH\"","description":"Check workspace contents and Swift version"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0126UgE6BZXzXs2WswUuaK4m","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Swift Package Manager structure 2025 iOS library Package.swift format"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LapdPXeMHXABZe8C17JHLG","type":"tool_result","content":"Swift version 6.0.3 (swift-6.0.3-RELEASE)\nTarget: aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu","is_error":false},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0126UgE6BZXzXs2WswUuaK4m","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Swift Package Manager structure 2025 iOS library Package.swift format\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"PackageDescription | Apple Developer Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/packagedescription\"},{\"title\":\"Swift Package Manager | Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.swift.org/swiftpm/documentation/packagemanagerdocs/\"},{\"title\":\"Package ‚Äî Swift Package Manager\",\"url\":\"https://docs.swift.org/package-manager/PackageDescription/PackageDescription.html\"},{\"title\":\"How to Use Swift Package Manager\",\"url\":\"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-02-02-swift-package-manager/view\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding Package.swift - Wesley de Groot\",\"url\":\"https://wesleydegroot.nl/blog/Understanding-Swift-Package\"},{\"title\":\"The Swift package manifest file - The.Swift.Dev.\",\"url\":\"https://theswiftdev.com/the-swift-package-manifest-file/\"},{\"title\":\"Mastering Swift Package Manager: A Comprehensive Guide | by Dipen Panchasara | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@dipenapanchasara/mastering-swift-package-manager-a-comprehensive-guide-5e06f29d812d\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - swiftlang/swift-format: Formatting technology for Swift source code ¬∑ GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/swiftlang/swift-format\"},{\"title\":\"Package.swift - Getting Started with the SPM Manifest file | BrightDigit\",\"url\":\"https://brightdigit.com/tutorials/swift-package-manifest-file/\"},{\"title\":\"Swift Package Manager Explained: Features, Setup and Best Practices\",\"url\":\"https://www.alliancetek.com/blog/post/2025/04/10/swift-package-manager-guide.aspx\"}]\n\nHere is a comprehensive overview of the **Swift Package Manager (SPM) structure for iOS libraries in 2025**, based on the latest documentation and guides:\n\n---\n\n## üì¶ Swift Package Manager ‚Äì iOS Library Structure & `Package.swift` Format (2025)\n\n---\n\n### üóÇÔ∏è Directory Structure\n\nWhen you initialize a new library package using `swift package init --type library`, SPM generates the following default file structure:\n\n```\nMySwiftPackage/\n‚îú‚îÄ‚îÄ Package.swift\n‚îú‚îÄ‚îÄ Sources/\n‚îÇ   ‚îî‚îÄ‚îÄ MySwiftPackage/\n‚îÇ       ‚îî‚îÄ‚îÄ MySwiftPackage.swift\n‚îî‚îÄ‚îÄ Tests/\n    ‚îî‚îÄ‚îÄ MySwiftPackageTests/\n        ‚îî‚îÄ‚îÄ MySwiftPackageTests.swift\n```\n\n\n---\n\n### üìÑ What is `Package.swift`?\n\n`Package.swift` is a **manifest file** that defines the structure and dependencies of a Swift package. It uses Swift syntax to describe the package's configuration, making it both powerful and easy to read. This file is crucial for managing dependencies, building libraries, and sharing code across different projects.\n\n---\n\n### üîë Key Sections of `Package.swift`\n\nA `Package.swift` manifest file **must begin** with the string `// swift-tools-version:` followed by a version number specifier.\n\nThe main sections are:\n\n1. **Package Description** ‚Äì The top-level structure that includes metadata about the package, such as its name, platforms, and Swift tools version.\n\n2. **Platforms (Optional)** ‚Äì Defines the platforms which your package supports.\n\n3. **Products** ‚Äì Defines the executables and libraries produced by the package. These can be used by other packages or applications.\n\n4. **Dependencies** ‚Äì Lists external packages that your package depends on. SPM will fetch and manage these dependencies for you.\n\n5. **Targets** ‚Äì The basic building blocks of a package. Each target can define a module or a test suite.\n\n6. **Resources (Optional)** ‚Äì Bundles resources with the package, such as images or sound files.\n\n---\n\n### üìù Example `Package.swift` for an iOS Library\n\nHere is a typical, well-structured `Package.swift` for a 2025 iOS library:\n\n```swift\n// swift-tools-version: 5.9\nimport PackageDescription\n\nlet package = Package(\n    name: \"NetworkKit\",\n    platforms: [\n        .iOS(.v15),\n        .macOS(.v12),\n        .tvOS(.v15),\n        .watchOS(.v8)\n    ],\n    products: [\n        .library(name: \"NetworkKit\", targets: [\"NetworkKit\"])\n    ],\n    dependencies: [\n        .package(url: \"https://url/of/another/package\", from: \"1.0.0\")\n    ],\n    targets: [\n        .target(\n            name: \"NetworkKit\",\n            dependencies: []\n        ),\n        .testTarget(\n            name: \"NetworkKitTests\",\n            dependencies: [\"NetworkKit\"]\n        )\n    ]\n)\n```\n\nThe `// swift-tools-version: 5.9` comment declares the minimum Swift version required. The `platforms` array restricts where your package can be used (e.g., `.iOS(.v15)`, `.macOS(.v12)`, `.tvOS(.v15)`, `.watchOS(.v8)`).\n\n---\n\n### üîß Products vs. Targets\n\nTo clarify the key difference: **Targets are internal; Products are external** (i.e., available to anyone). Targets can depend on other targets; products can contain multiple targets; and targets can depend on dependency products. Typically you'll have one product per target as well as a test target for your product.\n\n---\n\n### üåê Platform & Cross-Platform Support\n\nAn important feature of Swift Package Manager is its cross-platform support. SPM is designed to work across a variety of platforms, including macOS, iOS, watchOS, tvOS, and Linux. This makes it an ideal choice for developers building cross-platform applications using Swift.\n\n---\n\n### üîí Versioning & Dependency Resolution\n\nSPM supports **semantic versioning**, which allows developers to specify precise versions or version ranges for each dependency. This ensures compatibility and avoids issues from using incompatible versions of libraries. When a project has multiple dependencies, SPM automatically resolves them to ensure all required versions are compatible.\n\n---\n\n### ‚úÖ Key Advantages of SPM in 2025\n\nSwift Package Manager (SPM) is an essential tool in modern iOS development, offering a standardized and efficient approach to dependency management. It simplifies the integration of external libraries, encourages code reuse, and seamlessly aligns with the Swift ecosystem.\n\n- **Xcode Integration** ‚Äì Xcode has built-in support for SPM, making it easy to add and manage packages directly within your project.\n- **Version Control** ‚Äì With precise version control, SPM enables developers to specify compatible releases, minimizing version conflicts and enhancing project stability.\n- **Tools Version Compatibility** ‚Äì The Swift tools version declares the minimum version of the `PackageDescription` library required to process the manifest, and each version of Swift can introduce updates to the framework while the previous API version will continue to be available.\n\n---\n\nThis structure remains the standard for iOS library development going into 2025, with `swift-tools-version: 5.9` (or `6.0` for the latest Swift 6 features) being common in modern packages.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.\n\n<system-reminder>\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so m‚Ä¶\n- simplify: Review changed code for reuse, quality, and efficiency, then fix any issues found.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo, defaults to 10m) - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep‚Ä¶\n- claude-api: Build apps with the Claude API or Anthropic SDK.\nTRIGGER when: code imports `anthropic`/`@anthropic-ai/sdk`/`claude_agent_sdk`, or user asks to use Claude API, Anthropic SDKs, or Agent SDK.\nDO NOT TRIGGER when: code imports `openai`/other AI SDK, ge‚Ä¶\n</system-reminder>\n\n<system-reminder>\nPlan mode is active. The user indicated that they do not want you to execute yet -- you MUST NOT make any edits, run any non-readonly tools (including changing configs or making commits), or otherwise make any changes to the system. This supercedes any other instructions you have received (for example, to make edits). Instead, you should:\n\n## Plan File Info:\nNo plan file exists yet. You should create your plan at /root/.claude/plans/delegated-purring-book-agent-aa442e8656b7ff6a6.md using the Write tool if you need to.\nYou should build your plan incrementally by writing to or editing this file. NOTE that this is the only file you are allowed to edit - other than this you are only allowed to take READ-ONLY actions.\nAnswer the user's query comprehensively, using the AskUserQuestion tool if you need to ask the user clarifying questions. If you do use the AskUserQuestion, make sure to ask all clarifying questions you need to fully understand the user's intent before proceeding.\n</system-reminder>"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xdwo4Lcm2Ey8vG82Gkkm1e","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Swift 6 actor concurrency FileManager iOS Data protection FileAttributeKey.protectionKey"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XMLURh8q9S9MjaLc1uFYov","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Swift CryptoKit SHA256 hash string filename safe 2024"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xdwo4Lcm2Ey8vG82Gkkm1e","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Swift 6 actor concurrency FileManager iOS Data protection FileAttributeKey.protectionKey\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Efficient and Secure File Handling in iOS Swift: Best Practices, Encryption, API Integration, and Real-Time Solutions | by Kalidoss Shanmugam | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@kalidoss.shanmugam/efficient-and-secure-file-handling-in-ios-swift-best-practices-encryption-api-integration-and-b7d049b25bd9\"},{\"title\":\"iOS Dev Nugget 216 File Data Protection\",\"url\":\"https://hboon.com/iosdevnuggets/ios-dev-nugget-216-file-data-protection/\"},{\"title\":\"How to encrypt SwiftData - a free SwiftData by Example tutorial\",\"url\":\"https://www.hackingwithswift.com/quick-start/swiftdata/how-to-encrypt-swiftdata\"},{\"title\":\"FileProtectionType | Apple Developer Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/fileprotectiontype\"},{\"title\":\"SwiftyBeaver/Sources/FileDestination.swift at master ¬∑ SwiftyBeaver/SwiftyBeaver\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/SwiftyBeaver/SwiftyBeaver/blob/master/Sources/FileDestination.swift\"},{\"title\":\"Cannot convert value of type '[FileAttributeKey : Any]' to expected argument type '[String : Any]?' ¬∑ Issue #51 ¬∑ nvzqz/FileKit\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/nvzqz/FileKit/issues/51\"},{\"title\":\"swift-foundation/Sources/FoundationEssentials/FileManager/FileManager+Files.swift at main ¬∑ swiftlang/swift-foundation\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/swiftlang/swift-foundation/blob/main/Sources/FoundationEssentials/FileManager/FileManager+Files.swift\"},{\"title\":\"protectionKey | Apple Developer Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/fileattributekey/protectionkey\"},{\"title\":\"How to use iOS Data Protection (updated for iOS 17)\",\"url\":\"https://pspdfkit.com/blog/2017/how-to-use-ios-data-protection/\"},{\"title\":\"Swift 6 Concurrency | Axiom\",\"url\":\"https://charleswiltgen.github.io/Axiom/skills/concurrency/swift-concurrency.html\"}]\n\nHere's a comprehensive summary of what the search results reveal about using `FileManager`, `FileAttributeKey.protectionKey`, and iOS Data Protection in the context of Swift 6 and actor concurrency:\n\n---\n\n## üìÅ iOS Data Protection with `FileAttributeKey.protectionKey`\n\n### What It Is\n\n`FileAttributeKey.protectionKey` is the key in a file attribute dictionary whose value identifies the protection level for a file. The corresponding values are `FileProtectionType` ‚Äî protection level values that can be associated with a file attribute key.\n\n---\n\n### Setting File Protection on a File\n\nYou can set file protection using `FileManager.setAttributes(_:ofItemAtPath:)`:\n\n```swift\nfunc setFileProtection(fileName: String, protectionType: FileProtectionType) {\n    let fileManager = FileManager.default\n    if let documentDirectory = fileManager.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first {\n        let fileURL = documentDirectory.appendingPathComponent(fileName)\n        do {\n            try fileManager.setAttributes([.protectionKey: protectionType], ofItemAtPath: fileURL.path)\n        } catch {\n            print(\"Failed to set file protection: \\(error)\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIf you want to set a default protection class for every file your app creates, you can specify the `com.apple.developer.default-data-protection` entitlement in Xcode.\n\nFile protection is easy to implement and hardware-optimized ‚Äî you should use it.\n\n---\n\n### Setting Protection on a Directory\n\nYou can also set file protection when creating a directory:\n```swift\ntry FileManager.default.createDirectory(\n    at: directoryURL,\n    withIntermediateDirectories: true,\n    attributes: [.protectionKey: FileProtectionType.complete]\n)\n```\n\n> ‚ö†Ô∏è **Important:** When you set the protection level of a directory, it doesn't automatically apply to existing files in that directory ‚Äî you'll need to update the protection level for each individual file.\n\n---\n\n### iOS 17 Behavior Change\n\niOS 17 introduced new behavior with the `.atomic` option when creating files. Now, files created with `.atomic` inherit the protection level of the directory they're created in, which addresses the issue present in earlier versions where files would inherit protection from the temporary directory instead of the target directory.\n\n---\n\n### Using with SwiftData / Core Data\n\nYou can apply file protection to a SwiftData store:\n```swift\nif let path = modelContext.container.configurations.first?.url.path(percentEncoded: false) {\n    let attrs = [FileAttributeKey.protectionKey: FileProtectionType.completeUnlessOpen]\n    try? FileManager.default.setAttributes(attrs, ofItemAtPath: path)\n}\n```\nYou may also need to enable the Data Protection capability for your app in Xcode.\n\nHowever, this approach is more complicated than it might appear: the underlying data store is likely split into multiple files to support write-ahead logging, and SQLite can delete and recreate files at will as part of its self-healing mechanisms.\n\nIf you're supporting Core Data at the same time, you should adjust the `NSPersistentStoreFileProtectionKey` option key to match whatever you enabled for SwiftData.\n\n---\n\n### Internal Swift Foundation Handling (Swift Open Source)\n\nIn the Swift Foundation source, creating a file with a specific file protection class must have that class specified at `open()` time ‚Äî `NSFileProtectionKey` is special-cased so that it can be passed as an `NSDataWritingOption` instead.\n\n---\n\n### Swift 6 Concurrency Considerations\n\nSwift 6 strict concurrency patterns involve a progressive journey from single-threaded to concurrent code, including actor isolation, `Sendable`, `async/await`, and data race prevention.\n\nWhen working with `FileManager` in Swift 6 actors, keep these points in mind:\n\n- **`FileManager` is not `Sendable`** ‚Äî avoid sharing a single `FileManager` instance across actor boundaries. Use `FileManager.default` within isolated contexts or create a new instance per call.\n- **Actor isolation** ‚Äî wrap `FileManager` file protection calls inside an `actor` or `nonisolated` async function to avoid data races:\n\n```swift\nactor FileProtectionManager {\n    func applyProtection(to url: URL) throws {\n        try FileManager.default.setAttributes(\n            [.protectionKey: FileProtectionType.completeUntilFirstUserAuthentication],\n            ofItemAtPath: url.path\n        )\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- **Testing limitation:** Data Protection features do not function in the iOS Simulator ‚Äî testing should be conducted on actual devices.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of `FileProtectionType` Values\n\n| Value | Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `.complete` | File inaccessible when device is locked |\n| `.completeUnlessOpen` | File accessible only if already open when device locks |\n| `.completeUntilFirstUserAuthentication` | File accessible after first unlock until reboot |\n| `.none` | No protection |\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XMLURh8q9S9MjaLc1uFYov","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Swift CryptoKit SHA256 hash string filename safe 2024\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"How to calculate the SHA hash of a String or Data instance - free Swift example code and tips\",\"url\":\"https://www.hackingwithswift.com/example-code/cryptokit/how-to-calculate-the-sha-hash-of-a-string-or-data-instance\"},{\"title\":\"Common cryptographic operations in Swift with CryptoKit | by Dimitri James Tsiflitzis | The Startup | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/swlh/common-cryptographic-operations-in-swift-with-cryptokit-b30a4becc895\"},{\"title\":\"How to use cryptographic hash functions in CryptoKit for iOS security\",\"url\":\"https://tanaschita.com/20220105-using-hash-functions-in-swift/\"},{\"title\":\"Swift | CryptoKit in iOS: Simple and Secure (SHA256-AES.GCM) | by Berkan √áalƒ±≈ükan | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@berkancaliskan/swift-cryptokit-in-ios-simple-and-secure-sha256-aes-gcm-e61cb8f5b052\"},{\"title\":\"SHA-256 in Swift | Hashing and Validation in Multiple Programming Languages\",\"url\":\"https://ssojet.com/hashing/sha-256-in-swift\"},{\"title\":\"How to generate secure Hash in Swift using algorithms like SHA256, SHA384, and SHA512 | by Mykhailo Moiseienko | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://m-mois.medium.com/how-to-generate-secure-hashes-in-swift-using-algorithms-like-sha256-sha384-and-sha512-7cd0c979b57c\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding String Encoding using SHA-256 in Swift | by Harsha Agarwal | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@harshaag99/understanding-string-encoding-using-sha-256-in-swift-4a63672d8355\"},{\"title\":\"SHA256 | Apple Developer Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/cryptokit/sha256\"},{\"title\":\"Common Cryptographic Operations With CryptoKit - Andy Ibanez\",\"url\":\"https://www.andyibanez.com/posts/common-cryptographic-operations-with-cryptokit/\"},{\"title\":\"CryptoSwift | CryptoSwift is a growing collection of standard and secure cryptographic algorithms implemented in Swift\",\"url\":\"https://cryptoswift.io/\"}]\n\nHere's a comprehensive summary of the search results on **Swift CryptoKit SHA256 hashing for filename-safe strings**:\n\n---\n\n## üîê Swift CryptoKit: SHA256 Hash to a Filename-Safe String\n\n### Overview\n\niOS 13 introduced a new framework called **CryptoKit**, which adds important cryptographic functionality such as encryption and hashing. Cryptographic hash functions are essential tools in secure application development, commonly used for verifying data integrity, securely storing passwords, and generating unique identifiers. Apple's CryptoKit framework offers a range of hashing functions that are efficient, secure, and easy to implement within Swift.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Convert String to Data\n\nIf you want to calculate the hash value of a string, you need to convert it to an instance of `Data`, like this:\n```swift\nlet inputString = \"Hello, world!\"\nlet inputData = Data(inputString.utf8)\n```\nYou then call the `hash(data:)` method of whichever kind of hash you want: SHA-256, SHA-384, or SHA-512.\n\n> ‚ö†Ô∏è A common mistake is attempting to hash a string directly without this conversion. This will result in a compilation error because the `SHA256.hash` function specifically expects `Data`. Always ensure your input is a `Data` instance before hashing.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Compute the SHA-256 Hash\n\n`SHA256` is an implementation of Secure Hashing Algorithm 2 (SHA-2) hashing with a 256-bit digest.\n\n```swift\nimport CryptoKit\n\nlet data = Data(inputString.utf8)\nlet digest = SHA256.hash(data: data)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Convert Hash to a Filename-Safe Hex String\n\nSHA-256 hashes generated by CryptoKit are returned as a `SHA256.Digest` type, which is essentially a `ContiguousArray<UInt8>`. This raw byte representation is efficient for computation but not directly human-readable.\n\nTo make it **filename-safe**, convert it to a **lowercase hexadecimal string** (hex strings only contain `[0-9a-f]`, which are fully safe for filenames):\n\n\n```swift\nimport CryptoKit\n\nlet text = \"Hello CryptoKit\"\nlet data = Data(text.utf8)\nlet hash = SHA256.hash(data: data)\nlet hashString = hash.map { String(format: \"%02hhx\", $0) }.joined()\n// Converts each byte to a 2-digit hexadecimal string\n// Ensures leading zeros for consistent hash output\nprint(hashString)\n```\n\n\nThe `%02hhx` format specifier ensures:\n- **`02`** ‚Üí zero-pads to 2 digits (important for leading zeros)\n- **`hh`** ‚Üí treats the value as `unsigned char`\n- **`x`** ‚Üí outputs lowercase hex\n\n> üí° Prefer the `.map` method to obtain a hash representation because `print(digest)` uses the `description` property which Apple can change at any time, and that could break your code.\n\n---\n\n### Full Reusable Function\n\n```swift\nimport CryptoKit\nimport Foundation\n\nfunc sha256HashString(for input: String) -> String {\n    let data = Data(input.utf8)\n    let digest = SHA256.hash(data: data)\n    return digest.map { String(format: \"%02hhx\", $0) }.joined()\n}\n\n// Usage\nlet filename = sha256HashString(for: \"my_file_content_or_identifier\")\nprint(filename)\n// e.g. \"dffd6021bb2bd5b0af676290809ec3a53191dd81c7f70a4b28688a362182986f\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Hashing Files (Bonus)\n\nSHA-256 in Swift is incredibly versatile, capable of hashing any `Data` object. This extends directly to file contents, allowing you to generate a unique fingerprint for any file. To hash a file, you first read its contents into a `Data` object and then pass that data to the `SHA256.hash(data:)` method.\n\n> ‚ö†Ô∏è Loading very large files entirely into `Data` can consume significant system memory. If you're dealing with multi-gigabyte files, consider processing them in smaller, manageable chunks instead. This memory-efficient strategy prevents potential crashes and ensures smooth operation.\n\n---\n\n### Security Note\n\nWhile SHA-256 is excellent for verifying data integrity, its speed makes it unsuitable for **direct password storage**. A fast hash means attackers can quickly try millions of password combinations against your stored hashes. For passwords, use a KDF like PBKDF2 instead.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HL8K5Ckfh5xFDQEU5fsQmk","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Swift actor sendable Swift 6 strict concurrency nonisolated async init pattern"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V1VtmayZ3YPPF15FkVuABu","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Swift debounce Task cancel recreate pattern async await 2024"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V1VtmayZ3YPPF15FkVuABu","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Swift debounce Task cancel recreate pattern async await 2024\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Throttling or debouncing async sequences? - Using Swift - Swift Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.swift.org/t/throttling-or-debouncing-async-sequences/49751\"},{\"title\":\"Debounce in modern Swift - Using Swift - Swift Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.swift.org/t/debounce-in-modern-swift/83835\"},{\"title\":\"Debouncing with Swift concurrency - Side Effect\",\"url\":\"https://sideeffect.io/posts/2023-01-11-regulate/\"},{\"title\":\"Yielding and debouncing in Swift Concurrency | Swift with Majid\",\"url\":\"https://swiftwithmajid.com/2025/02/18/yielding-and-debouncing-in-swift-concurrency/\"},{\"title\":\"swift-async-algorithms/Sources/AsyncAlgorithms/AsyncAlgorithms.docc/Guides/Debounce.md at main ¬∑ apple/swift-async-algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/apple/swift-async-algorithms/blob/main/Sources/AsyncAlgorithms/AsyncAlgorithms.docc/Guides/Debounce.md\"},{\"title\":\"Debounce in Swift ¬∑ Tarka Labs - Tarka Labs\",\"url\":\"https://tarkalabs.com/blogs/debounce-in-swift/\"},{\"title\":\"üõÇ Modern Swift Concurrency in the Wild: AsyncStream, AsyncSequence & Cancellation Headaches | by Wesley Matlock | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@wesleymatlock/modern-swift-concurrency-in-the-wild-asyncstream-asyncsequence-cancelation-headaches-60722d779ccf\"},{\"title\":\"Emerge Tools Blog | Async await in Swift: The Full Toolkit\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergetools.com/blog/posts/swift-async-await-the-full-toolkit\"},{\"title\":\"Debounce with async/await & AsyncAlgorithms in SwiftUI and Observable macro | by Lucas Abijmil | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@lucas.abijmil/debounce-with-async-await-asyncalgorithms-in-swiftui-and-observable-macro-5a6169407326\"},{\"title\":\"How to use Debounce in SwiftUI or in Observable classes ‚Üí Livsy Code\",\"url\":\"https://livsycode.com/swiftui/how-to-use-debounce-in-swiftui-or-in-observable-classes/\"}]\n\nHere's a comprehensive overview of the **Swift Debounce: Task Cancel & Recreate Pattern** using `async/await`:\n\n---\n\n## üîÅ The Core Pattern: Task Cancel & Recreate\n\nThe fundamental idea behind debouncing with Swift Concurrency is simple:\n\n> **Cancel the previous `Task`, create a new one, sleep for the debounce duration, and only proceed if the task wasn't cancelled again.**\n\nWhen `emit(_:)` is called, the previous task is cancelled and a new `Task` is created that sleeps for the debounce duration before executing the block.\n\nThe previous task is cancelled, causing `Task.sleep(_:)` to throw an exception ‚Äî cleanly stopping execution.\n\n---\n\n## ‚úÖ Simple Implementation (Class-based)\n\n```swift\nfinal class Debounce<T> {\n    private let block: @Sendable (T) async -> Void\n    private let duration: ContinuousClock.Duration\n    private var task: Task<Void, Never>?\n\n    init(duration: ContinuousClock.Duration, block: @Sendable @escaping (T) async -> Void) {\n        self.duration = duration\n        self.block = block\n    }\n\n    func emit(value: T) {\n        self.task?.cancel()\n        self.task = Task { [duration, block] in\n            do {\n                try await Task.sleep(for: duration)\n                await block(value)\n            } catch {}\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis class is generic over `T` to allow debouncing any data type. The block is marked as `Sendable` because, as it is executed within a `Task`, it may run concurrently and must be thread-safe to prevent race conditions. The block is also marked as `async` ‚Äî affordable since we're already in the context of a `Task`.\n\n---\n\n## üßµ Actor-Based Implementation (Thread-Safe)\n\nDefining `Debounce` as an `actor` ensures thread safety. Debouncing is a core technique in responsive UI design. For years, Combine's `.debounce` has been the go-to solution, but in today's SwiftUI world where the Observation framework is replacing `@Published`, we need modern tools that play well with Swift Concurrency.\n\n```swift\nactor Debounce<each Parameter: Sendable>: Sendable {\n    private let action: @Sendable (repeat each Parameter) async -> Void\n    private let delay: Duration\n    private var task: Task<Void, Never>?\n\n    func callAsFunction(_ parameter: repeat each Parameter) {\n        task?.cancel()\n        task = Task {\n            try? await Task.sleep(for: delay)\n            guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }\n            await action(repeat each parameter)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nYou wrap an async function inside a `Debounce` instance. Every call is debounced ‚Äî only the last invocation is actually executed, after a specified delay. It uses Swift's `Task.sleep` for timing and cancellation for clean behavior.\n\n---\n\n## üñºÔ∏è SwiftUI: `.task(id:)` Pattern\n\nSwiftUI provides a built-in way to debounce using the `.task(id:)` modifier:\n\nUsing the `.task` view modifier with the `id` parameter starts a new task on every keystroke and cancels the previous one.\n\nSwift Concurrency doesn't provide a particular function for debouncing tasks, but you can easily implement it using `Task.sleep`. All you need to do is call `sleep` for some duration before running your heavy job. Whenever a task is cancelled while sleeping, it throws an error and interrupts the execution without running the heavy job ‚Äî reducing the amount of work done to display final results.\n\n```swift\n.task(id: query) {\n    do {\n        try await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(1))\n        await store.search(matching: query)\n    } catch {\n        // Task cancelled due to new query or view disappearance\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## üì¶ Using Swift Async Algorithms\n\nFor `AsyncSequence`-based pipelines, Apple's **swift-async-algorithms** package offers a native `.debounce()` operator:\n\nThe package contains algorithms that affect timing of values, such as `throttle()` and `debounce()`, algorithms that combine multiple sequences such as `zip()` or `combineLatest()`, and algorithms that modify inputs like `compacted()`, `adjacentPairs()`, or `removeDuplicates()`.\n\nIf values are produced by the base `AsyncSequence` during the quiet period, the debounce does not resume its next iterator until the period has elapsed with no values produced, or unless a terminal event is encountered.\n\n```swift\nfor await value in sequence\n    .debounce(for: .seconds(1)) {\n    handle(value)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## ‚ö†Ô∏è Resource Consideration\n\nAlthough the task-cancel-recreate solution works, it may not be the most resource-efficient. When processing a large number of events, creating numerous tasks can potentially strain system resources. To optimize, you may want a `Task` whose lifespan is extended every time an event occurs before the duration has elapsed ‚Äî rather than a separate `Task` for each event, a single longer-lasting `Task` for bursts of events.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Approach | Best For | Thread Safe |\n|---|---|---|\n| Class + `task?.cancel()` | Simple use cases | ‚ö†Ô∏è Needs care |\n| `actor`-based Debounce | General async functions | ‚úÖ Yes |\n| `.task(id:)` in SwiftUI | SwiftUI views | ‚úÖ Yes |\n| `AsyncAlgorithms .debounce()` | `AsyncSequence` pipelines | ‚úÖ Yes |\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HL8K5Ckfh5xFDQEU5fsQmk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Swift actor sendable Swift 6 strict concurrency nonisolated async init pattern\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Complete concurrency enabled by default ‚Äì available from Swift 6.0\",\"url\":\"https://www.hackingwithswift.com/swift/6.0/concurrency\"},{\"title\":\"Using Structured Concurrency and Shared State - Swift on server\",\"url\":\"https://swiftonserver.com/structured-concurrency-and-shared-state-in-swift/\"},{\"title\":\"Swift 6.2's Approachable Concurrency: What Actually Changes - Dominic Rodemer's Blog\",\"url\":\"https://blog.dominicrodemer.com/approachable-concurrency-in-swift-6-2/\"},{\"title\":\"Swift 6 Incomplete Migration Guide for Dummies | BrightDigit\",\"url\":\"https://brightdigit.com/tutorials/swift-6-async-await-actors-fixes/\"},{\"title\":\"Sendable warning for `nonisolated init` in `@MainActor` class even with Region based isolation ¬∑ Issue #73135 ¬∑ swiftlang/swift\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/swiftlang/swift/issues/73135\"},{\"title\":\"Approachable Concurrency in Swift 6.2: A Clear Guide\",\"url\":\"https://www.avanderlee.com/concurrency/approachable-concurrency-in-swift-6-2-a-clear-guide/\"},{\"title\":\"Swift Concurrency: Fixing Sendable, Actor Isolation, and Data Race Errors | by Ankur Chauhan | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@ankuriosdev/swift-concurrency-fixing-sendable-actor-isolation-and-data-race-errors-fc83d2d4e145\"},{\"title\":\"swift-evolution/proposals/0327-actor-initializers.md at main ¬∑ swiftlang/swift-evolution\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/swiftlang/swift-evolution/blob/main/proposals/0327-actor-initializers.md\"},{\"title\":\"XCTest Meets @MainActor: How to Fix Strict Concurrency Warnings\",\"url\":\"https://qualitycoding.org/xctest-mainactor/\"},{\"title\":\"Concurrency | Apple Developer Forums\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/forums/tags/concurrency?page=4\"}]\n\nHere's a comprehensive summary of what the search results reveal about **Swift actors, `Sendable`, Swift 6 strict concurrency, `nonisolated`, and `async init` patterns**:\n\n---\n\n## üîí Swift 6 Strict Concurrency & `Sendable`\n\nBefore Swift 6, the compiler was very strict: if you had a non-sendable value on one actor and tried to send it to another actor, you'd get concurrency checking warnings.\n\nSendable objects are now either those that conform to `Sendable`, or those that don't need to conform to `Sendable` because the compiler can prove they are being used safely ‚Äî it's a dramatic simplification of concurrency for developers.\n\nSendability is a contract, initiated by the type, that the type is safe to be sent between tasks. Structs and enums are automatically `Sendable` if all of their properties are `Sendable`. Since value types are copied when they are sent between tasks, you can safely send a struct or enum between tasks without worrying about race conditions.\n\nClasses are **not** automatically `Sendable`. Since reference types are explicitly not copied but referenced, you can't safely send a class between tasks.\n\n---\n\n## üé≠ Actor Isolation & `nonisolated`\n\n`nonisolated` fits when you've created an actor or a type marked for a global actor such as `@MainActor`. Every call made to that object will automatically be marked as `async`, since it needs to wait for that actor to be available. By marking a method as `nonisolated`, you allow it to be called from *outside* that actor.\n\nYou can use the `nonisolated` keyword to mark a function as lacking isolation. This allows you to access these functions without the `await` keyword and conform to protocols that have non-isolated methods.\n\n**Example with an actor using `nonisolated`:**\nAn actor can use `nonisolated` for protocol conformance methods, such as `nonisolated func makeAsyncIterator()`. Starting with Swift 5.10, `nonisolated(unsafe)` can be used to opt-out of actor isolation checking for stored properties.\n\n---\n\n## üèóÔ∏è `nonisolated` `init` Pattern in Actors\n\nDelegating initializers are conceptually a good place to construct a fresh instance of a non-`Sendable` value to pass along to the actor during initialization. This could only work by saying that only `nonisolated` self delegating initializers can accept a non-`Sendable` value from any context.\n\nCalling an isolated method from an initializer with a `nonisolated self` is permitted, provided that you can `await` the call. That call is considered a nonisolated use ‚Äî it's the first use of `self` other than to access a stored property. Afterwards, access to most stored properties within the `init` is lost, just like for the non-async case.\n\nA common pattern (from the Swift Evolution proposal) looks like:\n```swift\nclass NonSendable { init() {} }\n\n@MainActor class IsolatedType {\n    let a: NonSendable\n    // Create off the main thread using nonisolated init\n    nonisolated init() {\n        a = NonSendable()\n    }\n}\n```\nThis pattern ‚Äî a `@MainActor` class with a `nonisolated init()` ‚Äî allows you to initialize the type off the main thread, with no compiler warnings (in newer toolchains).\n\n---\n\n## üîÑ `nonisolated async` Functions & Swift 6.2 Changes\n\nIn Swift 6.1 and earlier, when you called a `nonisolated async` function from the main actor, it would hop to a background thread ‚Äî even if it didn't need to, even if you were just awaiting some data and updating a property.\n\nSwift 6.2 changes this default. Now, `nonisolated async` functions **inherit the caller's isolation**. If you call them from the main actor, they run on the main actor.\n\nIn short: Swift 6.2 runs `nonisolated async` functions on the **caller's actor** by default, unless the function is explicitly marked `@concurrent`.\n\nThe new `@concurrent` attribute can be used to explicitly opt a function into background execution: `@concurrent func processImage(_ data: Data) async -> UIImage?` ‚Äî this tells Swift to run it off the caller's actor for heavy work.\n\n---\n\n## ‚úÖ Best Practices Summary\n\nKey guidelines:\n- Use **actors** for repositories instead of `Sendable` classes.\n- Make repository methods `async` so they safely cross actor boundaries.\n- Mark all models as `Sendable` (including nested ones).\n- Snapshot mutable state before sending into a `Task`.\n- Avoid unsafe hacks like `@unchecked Sendable`.\n\nIt's highly recommended to migrate and enable each upcoming Swift 6.2 feature individually for existing projects. This ensures you're not suddenly introducing unexpected behavior that causes code to run on a different thread than before.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HATq6MN6vbeHPcCRqMFeRP","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Swift JSONEncoder JSONDecoder Codable persist index file async FileManager atomic write pattern"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012nxaeQfpgQYopH4bs5Y92N","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Swift Package swift-testing XCTest iOS unit test async actor 2024 2025"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012nxaeQfpgQYopH4bs5Y92N","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Swift Package swift-testing XCTest iOS unit test async actor 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Unit testing async/await Swift code - SwiftLee\",\"url\":\"https://www.avanderlee.com/concurrency/unit-testing-async-await/\"},{\"title\":\"Hello Swift Testing, Goodbye XCTest | by Leo | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://leocoout.medium.com/welcome-swift-testing-goodbye-xctest-7501b7a5b304\"},{\"title\":\"Swift Concurrency Testing: Writing Safe and Fast Async Unit Tests | by Commit Studio | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://commitstudiogs.medium.com/swift-concurrency-testing-writing-safe-and-fast-async-unit-tests-0a511117a4c4\"},{\"title\":\"How to test a method inside Task Async/await in swift - Swift Testing - Swift Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.swift.org/t/how-to-test-a-method-inside-task-async-await-in-swift/70686\"},{\"title\":\"Unit Testing Asynchronous Code in Swift\",\"url\":\"https://www.vadimbulavin.com/unit-testing-async-code-in-swift/\"},{\"title\":\"Async/await and XCTest - Using Swift - Swift Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.swift.org/t/async-await-and-xctest/44780\"},{\"title\":\"Asynchronous Tests and Expectations | Apple Developer Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xctest/asynchronous-tests-and-expectations\"},{\"title\":\"Unit testing Swift code that uses async/await | Swift by Sundell\",\"url\":\"https://www.swiftbysundell.com/articles/unit-testing-code-that-uses-async-await/\"},{\"title\":\"Async Unit Testing: The Comprehensive Guide\",\"url\":\"https://blog.jacobstechtavern.com/p/async-unit-testing-in-swift-the-comprehensive\"},{\"title\":\"Meet Swift Testing - WWDC24 - Videos - Apple Developer\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2024/10179/\"}]\n\nHere's a comprehensive summary of the latest developments around **Swift Testing**, **XCTest**, **async/await**, and **actors** for iOS unit testing in 2024‚Äì2025:\n\n---\n\n## üß™ Swift Testing (New in 2024)\n\nApple introduced **Swift Testing**, a new open-source package for testing Swift code, unveiled at WWDC 2024.\n\nIt includes powerful capabilities for describing and organizing tests, gives actionable details when failures occur, and scales to large codebases. Swift Testing was designed for Swift, embracing **modern features like concurrency and macros**, and supports all major platforms including Linux and Windows.\n\n### Swift Testing vs. XCTest\n\nTests in XCTest are any method whose name begins with `\"test\"`. But Swift Testing uses the `@Test` attribute to denote them explicitly, so there's no ambiguity. Swift Testing also supports more kinds of functions ‚Äî instance methods, static, or global functions.\n\nUnlike XCTest, Swift Testing supports **traits** for specifying information either per-test or per-suite. It also takes a different approach to parallelization: it **runs in-process using Swift Concurrency**, and supports physical devices like iPhone and Apple Watch.\n\nSwift Testing uses just two basic macros ‚Äî `#expect` and `#require` ‚Äî instead of many specialized functions. You can pass in ordinary expressions and language operators to `#expect` or `#require`, such as `==` to check equality or `>` to compare two values.\n\n### Syntax Comparison\n\nWith XCTest, you must import the `XCTest` framework, create a class inheriting from `XCTestCase`, and write test functions beginning with `test`. With Swift Testing, you import the `Testing` library and declare your function using the `@Test` macro.\n\n---\n\n## ‚ö° Async/Await in XCTest\n\nXCTest has had **direct support for async functions since Swift 5.5**. Simply designate your test method as `async`, and XCTest will handle the rest.\n\nNormally, creating a concurrent context involves wrapping async code within a `Task`, but **XCTest automatically does that wrapping work**, so you don't have to.\n\nThis lets you write asynchronous tests in a way that's almost identical to synchronous code. No more expectations, partial results, or managing timeouts.\n\nFor older callback-based code, you can still use `XCTestExpectation` with a timeout to prevent tests from hanging forever.\n\n---\n\n## üèÉ Parallelism & Actors\n\nSwift Testing **runs tests in parallel by default** for both sync and async tests, while XCTest only supports parallelization using multiple processes. If your XCTests are not prepared to run in parallel, you may need to add the `.serialized` trait everywhere.\n\nFor data safety, it's recommended to **avoid data races by using actors and `@MainActor`** in your async unit tests.\n\n---\n\n## ‚ö†Ô∏è Swift Testing Limitations (as of Early 2025)\n\nSwift Testing is still under active development and **does not yet support performance testing or UI Testing** (as of February 2025).\n\n---\n\n## üìö Key Resources\n\n- üé• [WWDC 2024 ‚Äì Meet Swift Testing](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2024/10179/)\n- üìñ [SwiftLee ‚Äì Unit Testing async/await](https://www.avanderlee.com/concurrency/unit-testing-async-await/)\n- üìñ [Swift by Sundell ‚Äì Async/Await Unit Testing](https://www.swiftbysundell.com/articles/unit-testing-code-that-uses-async-await/)\n- üìñ [Jacob's Tech Tavern ‚Äì Async Unit Testing Comprehensive Guide](https://blog.jacobstechtavern.com/p/async-unit-testing-in-swift-the-comprehensive)\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HATq6MN6vbeHPcCRqMFeRP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Swift JSONEncoder JSONDecoder Codable persist index file async FileManager atomic write pattern\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Encoding and decoding JSON in Swift - Ralf Ebert\",\"url\":\"https://www.ralfebert.com/ios/json-handling-in-swift/\"},{\"title\":\"Handling persistent data in SwiftUI 2.0 with JSON - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/arnavmotwani/handling-persistent-data-in-swiftui-2-0-with-json-1h7\"},{\"title\":\"How to Handle JSON Parsing with Codable in Swift\",\"url\":\"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-02-02-swift-codable-json/view\"},{\"title\":\"Use of Codable with JSONEncoder and JSONDecoder in Swift 4 | by Ashish Kakkad | Swift India | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/swift-india/use-of-codable-with-jsonencoder-and-jsondecoder-in-swift-4-71c3637a6c65\"},{\"title\":\"Storing Codable structs on the disk | Swift with Majid\",\"url\":\"https://swiftwithmajid.com/2019/05/22/storing-codable-structs-on-the-disk/\"},{\"title\":\"JSON Decoding in Swift with Codable: A Practical Guide\",\"url\":\"https://matteomanferdini.com/codable/\"},{\"title\":\"Stuff/Source/Codable/Codable.swift at master ¬∑ evermeer/Stuff\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/evermeer/Stuff/blob/master/Source/Codable/Codable.swift\"},{\"title\":\"Read and Write Codable Models from the JSON file inside FileManager. üéâ\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/ANSCoder/5bd0b8de8a6d4ad4338f0ff027609a24\"},{\"title\":\"Helper class to easily store and retrieve Codable structs from/to disk. https://medium.com/@sdrzn/swift-4-codable-lets-make-things-even-easier-c793b6cf29e1 ¬∑ GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/saoudrizwan/b7ab1febde724c6f30d8a555ea779140\"},{\"title\":\"Encoding and decoding data using Codable ‚Äì available from Swift 4.0\",\"url\":\"https://www.hackingwithswift.com/swift/4.0/codable\"}]\n\nHere is a comprehensive summary of the search results covering the **Swift `JSONEncoder`/`JSONDecoder` `Codable` persist index file async `FileManager` atomic write pattern**:\n\n---\n\n## üóÇÔ∏è Swift: Persisting Codable Data to Disk with FileManager & Atomic Writes\n\n### 1. üî§ The `Codable` Protocol\n\n`Codable` is a type alias that combines two protocols: `Encodable` and `Decodable`. Any type conforming to `Codable` can both encode itself to an external representation (like JSON) and decode from one.\n\n`Codable` is used to make your data types encodable and decodable for compatibility with external representations such as JSON. It supports both encoding and decoding by combining the `Encodable` and `Decodable` protocols.\n\nA minimal example:\n```swift\nstruct Person: Codable {\n    let name: String\n    let age: Int\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. ‚úçÔ∏è Encoding with `JSONEncoder`\n\n`JSONEncoder` supports `Codable` objects and produces JSON data that can be used to retrieve a JSON string.\n\n```swift\nlet encoder = JSONEncoder()\nlet jsonData = try encoder.encode(myObject)\n```\n\nWith that one tiny addition of `Codable` conformance, you can convert it to a `Data` representation of JSON using `JSONEncoder`. Swift will automatically encode all properties inside your data type ‚Äî you don't need to do anything.\n\n---\n\n### 3. üìñ Decoding with `JSONDecoder`\n\n`JSONDecoder` is an object that decodes instances of a data type from JSON objects, allowing you to get the object back from a JSON string.\n\n```swift\nlet decoder = JSONDecoder()\nlet myObject = try decoder.decode(MyType.self, from: jsonData)\n```\n\nA commonly configured decoder uses `keyDecodingStrategy = .convertFromSnakeCase` and `dateDecodingStrategy = .iso8601`, with the encoder set to matching strategies.\n\n---\n\n### 4. üíæ Writing to Disk with `FileManager` (Atomic Write Pattern)\n\nThe key to safely persisting a JSON index file is using **`.atomic`** write options, which ensures data integrity by writing to a temporary file first and then renaming it atomically:\n\nAn `ObservableObject` can write JSON data to the file manager like this ‚Äî first getting the document directory URL, then appending the filename and extension, and finally calling `JSONEncoder().encode(jsonArray).write(to: jsonURL, options: .atomic)`.\n\nThe atomic write pattern looks like this inside a `WritableStorage` extension:\n```swift\ntry value.write(to: url, options: .atomic)\n```\nIf the folders don't exist yet, `createDirectory(at:withIntermediateDirectories:attributes:)` is called first.\n\n---\n\n### 5. üèóÔ∏è A `CodableStorage` + `DiskStorage` Architecture\n\nFor a clean and reusable pattern, you can separate disk I/O from JSON coding:\n\nThe next step is implementing a simple adapter (`CodableStorage`) on top of `DiskStorage` to handle JSON coding/decoding, holding private instances of `JSONDecoder` and `JSONEncoder`.\n\nThe readable storage fetches data by constructing a URL from the storage root path and a given key (filename), using `FileManager.contents(atPath:)` to load the raw `Data`.\n\n---\n\n### 6. ‚ö° Async Reads and Writes\n\nFor background parsing of large payloads, you should move heavy parsing work off the main thread using `DispatchQueue.global(qos: .userInitiated).async`, dispatching results back to the main queue via a completion handler.\n\nAlternatively, using Swift Concurrency (`async/await`):\n```swift\nfunc saveIndex<T: Encodable>(_ index: T, to url: URL) async throws {\n    let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(index)\n    try await Task.detached(priority: .background) {\n        try data.write(to: url, options: .atomic)\n    }.value\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. üìÇ Choosing the Right Directory\n\nFiles you should put in the Caches directory include database cache files and downloadable content, such as that used by magazine, newspaper, and map applications.\n\nFor user-generated or persistent index data, prefer the **Documents directory**:\n```swift\nlet docDir = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first!\nlet indexURL = docDir.appendingPathComponent(\"index.json\")\n```\n\n---\n\n### 8. üß† Tips & Best Practices\n\n- Sometimes you don't need the complicated features of CoreData for simple JSON caching ‚Äî it is enough to implement disk storage.\n- If memory is a concern, avoid storing everything in a single large JSON file. You probably don't need all the data at once, so split it across separate files that you can read independently.\n- If you often need a specifically configured `JSONDecoder` in several places in your project, it's convenient to store such an instance in a static property inside an extension.\n\n---\n\nThis pattern ‚Äî `Codable` structs + `JSONEncoder`/`JSONDecoder` + `FileManager` + `.atomic` writes + async dispatch ‚Äî forms the foundation of a lightweight, safe, and efficient persistence layer for index files in Swift.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.","cache_control":{"type":"ephemeral"}}]}],"system":[{"type":"text","text":"x-anthropic-billing-header: cc_version=2.1.92.d5b; cc_entrypoint=cli; cch=27eeb;"},{"type":"text","text":"You are Claude Code, Anthropic's official CLI for Claude.","cache_control":{"type":"ephemeral"}},{"type":"text","text":"You are a software architect and planning specialist for Claude Code. Your role is to explore the codebase and design implementation plans.\n\n=== CRITICAL: READ-ONLY MODE - NO FILE MODIFICATIONS ===\nThis is a READ-ONLY planning task. You are STRICTLY PROHIBITED from:\n- Creating new files (no Write, touch, or file creation of any kind)\n- Modifying existing files (no Edit operations)\n- Deleting files (no rm or deletion)\n- Moving or copying files (no mv or cp)\n- Creating temporary files anywhere, including /tmp\n- Using redirect operators (>, >>, |) or heredocs to write to files\n- Running ANY commands that change system state\n\nYour role is EXCLUSIVELY to explore the codebase and design implementation plans. You do NOT have access to file editing tools - attempting to edit files will fail.\n\nYou will be provided with a set of requirements and optionally a perspective on how to approach the design process.\n\n## Your Process\n\n1. **Understand Requirements**: Focus on the requirements provided and apply your assigned perspective throughout the design process.\n\n2. **Explore Thoroughly**:\n   - Read any files provided to you in the initial prompt\n   - Find existing patterns and conventions using Glob, Grep, and Read\n   - Understand the current architecture\n   - Identify similar features as reference\n   - Trace through relevant code paths\n   - Use Bash ONLY for read-only operations (ls, git status, git log, git diff, find, cat, head, tail)\n   - NEVER use Bash for: mkdir, touch, rm, cp, mv, git add, git commit, npm install, pip install, or any file creation/modification\n\n3. **Design Solution**:\n   - Create implementation approach based on your assigned perspective\n   - Consider trade-offs and architectural decisions\n   - Follow existing patterns where appropriate\n\n4. **Detail the Plan**:\n   - Provide step-by-step implementation strategy\n   - Identify dependencies and sequencing\n   - Anticipate potential challenges\n\n## Required Output\n\nEnd your response with:\n\n### Critical Files for Implementation\nList 3-5 files most critical for implementing this plan:\n- path/to/file1.ts\n- path/to/file2.ts\n- path/to/file3.ts\n\nREMEMBER: You can ONLY explore and plan. You CANNOT and MUST NOT write, edit, or modify any files. You do NOT have access to file editing tools.\n\nNotes:\n- Agent threads always have their cwd reset between bash calls, as a result please only use absolute file paths.\n- In your final response, share file paths (always absolute, never relative) that are relevant to the task. Include code snippets only when the exact text is load-bearing (e.g., a bug you found, a function signature the caller asked for) ‚Äî do not recap code you merely read.\n- For clear communication with the user the assistant MUST avoid using emojis.\n- Do not use a colon before tool calls. Text like \"Let me read the file:\" followed by a read tool call should just be \"Let me read the file.\" with a period.\n\nHere is useful information about the environment you are running in:\n<env>\nWorking directory: /workspace\nIs directory a git repo: Yes\nPlatform: linux\nShell: unknown\nOS Version: Linux 6.10.14-linuxkit\n</env>\nYou are powered by the model named Sonnet 4.6. The exact model ID is claude-sonnet-4-6.\n\nAssistant knowledge cutoff is August 2025.","cache_control":{"type":"ephemeral"}}],"tools":[{"name":"Bash","description":"Executes a given bash command and returns its output.\n\nThe working directory persists between commands, but shell state does not. The shell environment is initialized from the user's profile (bash or zsh).\n\nIMPORTANT: Avoid using this tool to run `find`, `grep`, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `sed`, `awk`, or `echo` commands, unless explicitly instructed or after you have verified that a dedicated tool cannot accomplish your task. Instead, use the appropriate dedicated tool as this will provide a much better experience for the user:\n\n - File search: Use Glob (NOT find or ls)\n - Content search: Use Grep (NOT grep or rg)\n - Read files: Use Read (NOT cat/head/tail)\n - Edit files: Use Edit (NOT sed/awk)\n - Write files: Use Write (NOT echo >/cat <<EOF)\n - Communication: Output text directly (NOT echo/printf)\nWhile the Bash tool can do similar things, it‚Äôs better to use the built-in tools as they provide a better user experience and make it easier to review tool calls and give permission.\n\n# Instructions\n - If your command will create new directories or files, first use this tool to run `ls` to verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location.\n - Always quote file paths that contain spaces with double quotes in your command (e.g., cd \"path with spaces/file.txt\")\n - Try to maintain your current working directory throughout the session by using absolute paths and avoiding usage of `cd`. You may use `cd` if the User explicitly requests it.\n - You may specify an optional timeout in milliseconds (up to 600000ms / 10 minutes). By default, your command will timeout after 120000ms (2 minutes).\n - You can use the `run_in_background` parameter to run the command in the background. Only use this if you don't need the result immediately and are OK being notified when the command completes later. You do not need to check the output right away - you'll be notified when it finishes. You do not need to use '&' at the end of the command when using this parameter.\n - When issuing multiple commands:\n  - If the commands are independent and can run in parallel, make multiple Bash tool calls in a single message. Example: if you need to run \"git status\" and \"git diff\", send a single message with two Bash tool calls in parallel.\n  - If the commands depend on each other and must run sequentially, use a single Bash call with '&&' to chain them together.\n  - Use ';' only when you need to run commands sequentially but don't care if earlier commands fail.\n  - DO NOT use newlines to separate commands (newlines are ok in quoted strings).\n - For git commands:\n  - Prefer to create a new commit rather than amending an existing commit.\n  - Before running destructive operations (e.g., git reset --hard, git push --force, git checkout --), consider whether there is a safer alternative that achieves the same goal. Only use destructive operations when they are truly the best approach.\n  - Never skip hooks (--no-verify) or bypass signing (--no-gpg-sign, -c commit.gpgsign=false) unless the user has explicitly asked for it. If a hook fails, investigate and fix the underlying issue.\n - Avoid unnecessary `sleep` commands:\n  - Do not sleep between commands that can run immediately ‚Äî just run them.\n  - If your command is long running and you would like to be notified when it finishes ‚Äî use `run_in_background`. No sleep needed.\n  - Do not retry failing commands in a sleep loop ‚Äî diagnose the root cause.\n  - If waiting for a background task you started with `run_in_background`, you will be notified when it completes ‚Äî do not poll.\n  - If you must poll an external process, use a check command (e.g. `gh run view`) rather than sleeping first.\n  - If you must sleep, keep the duration short (1-5 seconds) to avoid blocking the user.\n\n\n# Committing changes with git\n\nOnly create commits when requested by the user. If unclear, ask first. When the user asks you to create a new git commit, follow these steps carefully:\n\nYou can call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested and all commands are likely to succeed, run multiple tool calls in parallel for optimal performance. The numbered steps below indicate which commands should be batched in parallel.\n\nGit Safety Protocol:\n- NEVER update the git config\n- NEVER run destructive git commands (push --force, reset --hard, checkout ., restore ., clean -f, branch -D) unless the user explicitly requests these actions. Taking unauthorized destructive actions is unhelpful and can result in lost work, so it's best to ONLY run these commands when given direct instructions \n- NEVER skip hooks (--no-verify, --no-gpg-sign, etc) unless the user explicitly requests it\n- NEVER run force push to main/master, warn the user if they request it\n- CRITICAL: Always create NEW commits rather than amending, unless the user explicitly requests a git amend. When a pre-commit hook fails, the commit did NOT happen ‚Äî so --amend would modify the PREVIOUS commit, which may result in destroying work or losing previous changes. Instead, after hook failure, fix the issue, re-stage, and create a NEW commit\n- When staging files, prefer adding specific files by name rather than using \"git add -A\" or \"git add .\", which can accidentally include sensitive files (.env, credentials) or large binaries\n- NEVER commit changes unless the user explicitly asks you to. It is VERY IMPORTANT to only commit when explicitly asked, otherwise the user will feel that you are being too proactive\n\n1. Run the following bash commands in parallel, each using the Bash tool:\n  - Run a git status command to see all untracked files. IMPORTANT: Never use the -uall flag as it can cause memory issues on large repos.\n  - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed.\n  - Run a git log command to see recent commit messages, so that you can follow this repository's commit message style.\n2. Analyze all staged changes (both previously staged and newly added) and draft a commit message:\n  - Summarize the nature of the changes (eg. new feature, enhancement to an existing feature, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.). Ensure the message accurately reflects the changes and their purpose (i.e. \"add\" means a wholly new feature, \"update\" means an enhancement to an existing feature, \"fix\" means a bug fix, etc.).\n  - Do not commit files that likely contain secrets (.env, credentials.json, etc). Warn the user if they specifically request to commit those files\n  - Draft a concise (1-2 sentences) commit message that focuses on the \"why\" rather than the \"what\"\n  - Ensure it accurately reflects the changes and their purpose\n3. Run the following commands in parallel:\n   - Add relevant untracked files to the staging area.\n   - Create the commit with a message ending with:\n   Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n   - Run git status after the commit completes to verify success.\n   Note: git status depends on the commit completing, so run it sequentially after the commit.\n4. If the commit fails due to pre-commit hook: fix the issue and create a NEW commit\n\nImportant notes:\n- NEVER run additional commands to read or explore code, besides git bash commands\n- NEVER use the TodoWrite or Agent tools\n- DO NOT push to the remote repository unless the user explicitly asks you to do so\n- IMPORTANT: Never use git commands with the -i flag (like git rebase -i or git add -i) since they require interactive input which is not supported.\n- IMPORTANT: Do not use --no-edit with git rebase commands, as the --no-edit flag is not a valid option for git rebase.\n- If there are no changes to commit (i.e., no untracked files and no modifications), do not create an empty commit\n- In order to ensure good formatting, ALWAYS pass the commit message via a HEREDOC, a la this example:\n<example>\ngit commit -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\n   Commit message here.\n\n   Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n   EOF\n   )\"\n</example>\n\n# Creating pull requests\nUse the gh command via the Bash tool for ALL GitHub-related tasks including working with issues, pull requests, checks, and releases. If given a Github URL use the gh command to get the information needed.\n\nIMPORTANT: When the user asks you to create a pull request, follow these steps carefully:\n\n1. Run the following bash commands in parallel using the Bash tool, in order to understand the current state of the branch since it diverged from the main branch:\n   - Run a git status command to see all untracked files (never use -uall flag)\n   - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed\n   - Check if the current branch tracks a remote branch and is up to date with the remote, so you know if you need to push to the remote\n   - Run a git log command and `git diff [base-branch]...HEAD` to understand the full commit history for the current branch (from the time it diverged from the base branch)\n2. Analyze all changes that will be included in the pull request, making sure to look at all relevant commits (NOT just the latest commit, but ALL commits that will be included in the pull request!!!), and draft a pull request title and summary:\n   - Keep the PR title short (under 70 characters)\n   - Use the description/body for details, not the title\n3. Run the following commands in parallel:\n   - Create new branch if needed\n   - Push to remote with -u flag if needed\n   - Create PR using gh pr create with the format below. Use a HEREDOC to pass the body to ensure correct formatting.\n<example>\ngh pr create --title \"the pr title\" --body \"$(cat <<'EOF'\n## Summary\n<1-3 bullet points>\n\n## Test plan\n[Bulleted markdown checklist of TODOs for testing the pull request...]\n\nü§ñ Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\nEOF\n)\"\n</example>\n\nImportant:\n- DO NOT use the TodoWrite or Agent tools\n- Return the PR URL when you're done, so the user can see it\n\n# Other common operations\n- View comments on a Github PR: gh api repos/foo/bar/pulls/123/comments","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"command":{"description":"The command to execute","type":"string"},"timeout":{"description":"Optional timeout in milliseconds (max 600000)","type":"number"},"description":{"description":"Clear, concise description of what this command does in active voice. Never use words like \"complex\" or \"risk\" in the description - just describe what it does.\n\nFor simple commands (git, npm, standard CLI tools), keep it brief (5-10 words):\n- ls ‚Üí \"List files in current directory\"\n- git status ‚Üí \"Show working tree status\"\n- npm install ‚Üí \"Install package dependencies\"\n\nFor commands that are harder to parse at a glance (piped commands, obscure flags, etc.), add enough context to clarify what it does:\n- find . -name \"*.tmp\" -exec rm {} \\; ‚Üí \"Find and delete all .tmp files recursively\"\n- git reset --hard origin/main ‚Üí \"Discard all local changes and match remote main\"\n- curl -s url | jq '.data[]' ‚Üí \"Fetch JSON from URL and extract data array elements\"","type":"string"},"run_in_background":{"description":"Set to true to run this command in the background. Use Read to read the output later.","type":"boolean"},"dangerouslyDisableSandbox":{"description":"Set this to true to dangerously override sandbox mode and run commands without sandboxing.","type":"boolean"}},"required":["command"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"CronCreate","description":"Schedule a prompt to be enqueued at a future time. Use for both recurring schedules and one-shot reminders.\n\nUses standard 5-field cron in the user's local timezone: minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week. \"0 9 * * *\" means 9am local ‚Äî no timezone conversion needed.\n\n## One-shot tasks (recurring: false)\n\nFor \"remind me at X\" or \"at <time>, do Y\" requests ‚Äî fire once then auto-delete.\nPin minute/hour/day-of-month/month to specific values:\n  \"remind me at 2:30pm today to check the deploy\" ‚Üí cron: \"30 14 <today_dom> <today_month> *\", recurring: false\n  \"tomorrow morning, run the smoke test\" ‚Üí cron: \"57 8 <tomorrow_dom> <tomorrow_month> *\", recurring: false\n\n## Recurring jobs (recurring: true, the default)\n\nFor \"every N minutes\" / \"every hour\" / \"weekdays at 9am\" requests:\n  \"*/5 * * * *\" (every 5 min), \"0 * * * *\" (hourly), \"0 9 * * 1-5\" (weekdays at 9am local)\n\n## Avoid the :00 and :30 minute marks when the task allows it\n\nEvery user who asks for \"9am\" gets `0 9`, and every user who asks for \"hourly\" gets `0 *` ‚Äî which means requests from across the planet land on the API at the same instant. When the user's request is approximate, pick a minute that is NOT 0 or 30:\n  \"every morning around 9\" ‚Üí \"57 8 * * *\" or \"3 9 * * *\" (not \"0 9 * * *\")\n  \"hourly\" ‚Üí \"7 * * * *\" (not \"0 * * * *\")\n  \"in an hour or so, remind me to...\" ‚Üí pick whatever minute you land on, don't round\n\nOnly use minute 0 or 30 when the user names that exact time and clearly means it (\"at 9:00 sharp\", \"at half past\", coordinating with a meeting). When in doubt, nudge a few minutes early or late ‚Äî the user will not notice, and the fleet will.\n\n## Durability\n\nBy default (durable: false) the job lives only in this Claude session ‚Äî nothing is written to disk, and the job is gone when Claude exits. Pass durable: true to write to .claude/scheduled_tasks.json so the job survives restarts. Only use durable: true when the user explicitly asks for the task to persist (\"keep doing this every day\", \"set this up permanently\"). Most \"remind me in 5 minutes\" / \"check back in an hour\" requests should stay session-only.\n\n## Runtime behavior\n\nJobs only fire while the REPL is idle (not mid-query). Durable jobs persist to .claude/scheduled_tasks.json and survive session restarts ‚Äî on next launch they resume automatically. One-shot durable tasks that were missed while the REPL was closed are surfaced for catch-up. Session-only jobs die with the process. The scheduler adds a small deterministic jitter on top of whatever you pick: recurring tasks fire up to 10% of their period late (max 15 min); one-shot tasks landing on :00 or :30 fire up to 90 s early. Picking an off-minute is still the bigger lever.\n\nRecurring tasks auto-expire after 7 days ‚Äî they fire one final time, then are deleted. This bounds session lifetime. Tell the user about the 7-day limit when scheduling recurring jobs.\n\nReturns a job ID you can pass to CronDelete.","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"cron":{"description":"Standard 5-field cron expression in local time: \"M H DoM Mon DoW\" (e.g. \"*/5 * * * *\" = every 5 minutes, \"30 14 28 2 *\" = Feb 28 at 2:30pm local once).","type":"string"},"prompt":{"description":"The prompt to enqueue at each fire time.","type":"string"},"recurring":{"description":"true (default) = fire on every cron match until deleted or auto-expired after 7 days. false = fire once at the next match, then auto-delete. Use false for \"remind me at X\" one-shot requests with pinned minute/hour/dom/month.","type":"boolean"},"durable":{"description":"true = persist to .claude/scheduled_tasks.json and survive restarts. false (default) = in-memory only, dies when this Claude session ends. Use true only when the user asks the task to survive across sessions.","type":"boolean"}},"required":["cron","prompt"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"CronDelete","description":"Cancel a cron job previously scheduled with CronCreate. Removes it from .claude/scheduled_tasks.json (durable jobs) or the in-memory session store (session-only jobs).","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"id":{"description":"Job ID returned by CronCreate.","type":"string"}},"required":["id"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"CronList","description":"List all cron jobs scheduled via CronCreate, both durable (.claude/scheduled_tasks.json) and session-only.","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"EnterWorktree","description":"Use this tool ONLY when the user explicitly asks to work in a worktree. This tool creates an isolated git worktree and switches the current session into it.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- The user explicitly says \"worktree\" (e.g., \"start a worktree\", \"work in a worktree\", \"create a worktree\", \"use a worktree\")\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n- The user asks to create a branch, switch branches, or work on a different branch ‚Äî use git commands instead\n- The user asks to fix a bug or work on a feature ‚Äî use normal git workflow unless they specifically mention worktrees\n- Never use this tool unless the user explicitly mentions \"worktree\"\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Must be in a git repository, OR have WorktreeCreate/WorktreeRemove hooks configured in settings.json\n- Must not already be in a worktree\n\n## Behavior\n\n- In a git repository: creates a new git worktree inside `.claude/worktrees/` with a new branch based on HEAD\n- Outside a git repository: delegates to WorktreeCreate/WorktreeRemove hooks for VCS-agnostic isolation\n- Switches the session's working directory to the new worktree\n- Use ExitWorktree to leave the worktree mid-session (keep or remove). On session exit, if still in the worktree, the user will be prompted to keep or remove it\n\n## Parameters\n\n- `name` (optional): A name for the worktree. If not provided, a random name is generated.\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"name":{"description":"Optional name for the worktree. Each \"/\"-separated segment may contain only letters, digits, dots, underscores, and dashes; max 64 chars total. A random name is generated if not provided.","type":"string"}},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"ExitWorktree","description":"Exit a worktree session created by EnterWorktree and return the session to the original working directory.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis tool ONLY operates on worktrees created by EnterWorktree in this session. It will NOT touch:\n- Worktrees you created manually with `git worktree add`\n- Worktrees from a previous session (even if created by EnterWorktree then)\n- The directory you're in if EnterWorktree was never called\n\nIf called outside an EnterWorktree session, the tool is a **no-op**: it reports that no worktree session is active and takes no action. Filesystem state is unchanged.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- The user explicitly asks to \"exit the worktree\", \"leave the worktree\", \"go back\", or otherwise end the worktree session\n- Do NOT call this proactively ‚Äî only when the user asks\n\n## Parameters\n\n- `action` (required): `\"keep\"` or `\"remove\"`\n  - `\"keep\"` ‚Äî leave the worktree directory and branch intact on disk. Use this if the user wants to come back to the work later, or if there are changes to preserve.\n  - `\"remove\"` ‚Äî delete the worktree directory and its branch. Use this for a clean exit when the work is done or abandoned.\n- `discard_changes` (optional, default false): only meaningful with `action: \"remove\"`. If the worktree has uncommitted files or commits not on the original branch, the tool will REFUSE to remove it unless this is set to `true`. If the tool returns an error listing changes, confirm with the user before re-invoking with `discard_changes: true`.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Restores the session's working directory to where it was before EnterWorktree\n- Clears CWD-dependent caches (system prompt sections, memory files, plans directory) so the session state reflects the original directory\n- If a tmux session was attached to the worktree: killed on `remove`, left running on `keep` (its name is returned so the user can reattach)\n- Once exited, EnterWorktree can be called again to create a fresh worktree\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"action":{"description":"\"keep\" leaves the worktree and branch on disk; \"remove\" deletes both.","type":"string","enum":["keep","remove"]},"discard_changes":{"description":"Required true when action is \"remove\" and the worktree has uncommitted files or unmerged commits. The tool will refuse and list them otherwise.","type":"boolean"}},"required":["action"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"Glob","description":"- Fast file pattern matching tool that works with any codebase size\n- Supports glob patterns like \"**/*.js\" or \"src/**/*.ts\"\n- Returns matching file paths sorted by modification time\n- Use this tool when you need to find files by name patterns\n- When you are doing an open ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Agent tool instead","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"pattern":{"description":"The glob pattern to match files against","type":"string"},"path":{"description":"The directory to search in. If not specified, the current working directory will be used. IMPORTANT: Omit this field to use the default directory. DO NOT enter \"undefined\" or \"null\" - simply omit it for the default behavior. Must be a valid directory path if provided.","type":"string"}},"required":["pattern"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"Grep","description":"A powerful search tool built on ripgrep\n\n  Usage:\n  - ALWAYS use Grep for search tasks. NEVER invoke `grep` or `rg` as a Bash command. The Grep tool has been optimized for correct permissions and access.\n  - Supports full regex syntax (e.g., \"log.*Error\", \"function\\s+\\w+\")\n  - Filter files with glob parameter (e.g., \"*.js\", \"**/*.tsx\") or type parameter (e.g., \"js\", \"py\", \"rust\")\n  - Output modes: \"content\" shows matching lines, \"files_with_matches\" shows only file paths (default), \"count\" shows match counts\n  - Use Agent tool for open-ended searches requiring multiple rounds\n  - Pattern syntax: Uses ripgrep (not grep) - literal braces need escaping (use `interface\\{\\}` to find `interface{}` in Go code)\n  - Multiline matching: By default patterns match within single lines only. For cross-line patterns like `struct \\{[\\s\\S]*?field`, use `multiline: true`\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"pattern":{"description":"The regular expression pattern to search for in file contents","type":"string"},"path":{"description":"File or directory to search in (rg PATH). Defaults to current working directory.","type":"string"},"glob":{"description":"Glob pattern to filter files (e.g. \"*.js\", \"*.{ts,tsx}\") - maps to rg --glob","type":"string"},"output_mode":{"description":"Output mode: \"content\" shows matching lines (supports -A/-B/-C context, -n line numbers, head_limit), \"files_with_matches\" shows file paths (supports head_limit), \"count\" shows match counts (supports head_limit). Defaults to \"files_with_matches\".","type":"string","enum":["content","files_with_matches","count"]},"-B":{"description":"Number of lines to show before each match (rg -B). Requires output_mode: \"content\", ignored otherwise.","type":"number"},"-A":{"description":"Number of lines to show after each match (rg -A). Requires output_mode: \"content\", ignored otherwise.","type":"number"},"-C":{"description":"Alias for context.","type":"number"},"context":{"description":"Number of lines to show before and after each match (rg -C). Requires output_mode: \"content\", ignored otherwise.","type":"number"},"-n":{"description":"Show line numbers in output (rg -n). Requires output_mode: \"content\", ignored otherwise. Defaults to true.","type":"boolean"},"-i":{"description":"Case insensitive search (rg -i)","type":"boolean"},"type":{"description":"File type to search (rg --type). Common types: js, py, rust, go, java, etc. More efficient than include for standard file types.","type":"string"},"head_limit":{"description":"Limit output to first N lines/entries, equivalent to \"| head -N\". Works across all output modes: content (limits output lines), files_with_matches (limits file paths), count (limits count entries). Defaults to 250 when unspecified. Pass 0 for unlimited (use sparingly ‚Äî large result sets waste context).","type":"number"},"offset":{"description":"Skip first N lines/entries before applying head_limit, equivalent to \"| tail -n +N | head -N\". Works across all output modes. Defaults to 0.","type":"number"},"multiline":{"description":"Enable multiline mode where . matches newlines and patterns can span lines (rg -U --multiline-dotall). Default: false.","type":"boolean"}},"required":["pattern"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"Read","description":"Reads a file from the local filesystem. You can access any file directly by using this tool.\nAssume this tool is able to read all files on the machine. If the User provides a path to a file assume that path is valid. It is okay to read a file that does not exist; an error will be returned.\n\nUsage:\n- The file_path parameter must be an absolute path, not a relative path\n- By default, it reads up to 2000 lines starting from the beginning of the file\n- You can optionally specify a line offset and limit (especially handy for long files), but it's recommended to read the whole file by not providing these parameters\n- Results are returned using cat -n format, with line numbers starting at 1\n- This tool allows Claude Code to read images (eg PNG, JPG, etc). When reading an image file the contents are presented visually as Claude Code is a multimodal LLM.\n- This tool can read PDF files (.pdf). For large PDFs (more than 10 pages), you MUST provide the pages parameter to read specific page ranges (e.g., pages: \"1-5\"). Reading a large PDF without the pages parameter will fail. Maximum 20 pages per request.\n- This tool can read Jupyter notebooks (.ipynb files) and returns all cells with their outputs, combining code, text, and visualizations.\n- This tool can only read files, not directories. To read a directory, use an ls command via the Bash tool.\n- You will regularly be asked to read screenshots. If the user provides a path to a screenshot, ALWAYS use this tool to view the file at the path. This tool will work with all temporary file paths.\n- If you read a file that exists but has empty contents you will receive a system reminder warning in place of file contents.","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"file_path":{"description":"The absolute path to the file to read","type":"string"},"offset":{"description":"The line number to start reading from. Only provide if the file is too large to read at once","type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"limit":{"description":"The number of lines to read. Only provide if the file is too large to read at once.","type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"pages":{"description":"Page range for PDF files (e.g., \"1-5\", \"3\", \"10-20\"). Only applicable to PDF files. Maximum 20 pages per request.","type":"string"}},"required":["file_path"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"Skill","description":"Execute a skill within the main conversation\n\nWhen users ask you to perform tasks, check if any of the available skills match. Skills provide specialized capabilities and domain knowledge.\n\nWhen users reference a \"slash command\" or \"/<something>\" (e.g., \"/commit\", \"/review-pr\"), they are referring to a skill. Use this tool to invoke it.\n\nHow to invoke:\n- Use this tool with the skill name and optional arguments\n- Examples:\n  - `skill: \"pdf\"` - invoke the pdf skill\n  - `skill: \"commit\", args: \"-m 'Fix bug'\"` - invoke with arguments\n  - `skill: \"review-pr\", args: \"123\"` - invoke with arguments\n  - `skill: \"ms-office-suite:pdf\"` - invoke using fully qualified name\n\nImportant:\n- Available skills are listed in system-reminder messages in the conversation\n- When a skill matches the user's request, this is a BLOCKING REQUIREMENT: invoke the relevant Skill tool BEFORE generating any other response about the task\n- NEVER mention a skill without actually calling this tool\n- Do not invoke a skill that is already running\n- Do not use this tool for built-in CLI commands (like /help, /clear, etc.)\n- If you see a <command-name> tag in the current conversation turn, the skill has ALREADY been loaded - follow the instructions directly instead of calling this tool again\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"skill":{"description":"The skill name. E.g., \"commit\", \"review-pr\", or \"pdf\"","type":"string"},"args":{"description":"Optional arguments for the skill","type":"string"}},"required":["skill"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"TaskCreate","description":"Use this tool to create a structured task list for your current coding session. This helps you track progress, organize complex tasks, and demonstrate thoroughness to the user.\nIt also helps the user understand the progress of the task and overall progress of their requests.\n\n## When to Use This Tool\n\nUse this tool proactively in these scenarios:\n\n- Complex multi-step tasks - When a task requires 3 or more distinct steps or actions\n- Non-trivial and complex tasks - Tasks that require careful planning or multiple operations\n- Plan mode - When using plan mode, create a task list to track the work\n- User explicitly requests todo list - When the user directly asks you to use the todo list\n- User provides multiple tasks - When users provide a list of things to be done (numbered or comma-separated)\n- After receiving new instructions - Immediately capture user requirements as tasks\n- When you start working on a task - Mark it as in_progress BEFORE beginning work\n- After completing a task - Mark it as completed and add any new follow-up tasks discovered during implementation\n\n## When NOT to Use This Tool\n\nSkip using this tool when:\n- There is only a single, straightforward task\n- The task is trivial and tracking it provides no organizational benefit\n- The task can be completed in less than 3 trivial steps\n- The task is purely conversational or informational\n\nNOTE that you should not use this tool if there is only one trivial task to do. In this case you are better off just doing the task directly.\n\n## Task Fields\n\n- **subject**: A brief, actionable title in imperative form (e.g., \"Fix authentication bug in login flow\")\n- **description**: What needs to be done\n- **activeForm** (optional): Present continuous form shown in the spinner when the task is in_progress (e.g., \"Fixing authentication bug\"). If omitted, the spinner shows the subject instead.\n\nAll tasks are created with status `pending`.\n\n## Tips\n\n- Create tasks with clear, specific subjects that describe the outcome\n- After creating tasks, use TaskUpdate to set up dependencies (blocks/blockedBy) if needed\n- Check TaskList first to avoid creating duplicate tasks\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"subject":{"description":"A brief title for the task","type":"string"},"description":{"description":"What needs to be done","type":"string"},"activeForm":{"description":"Present continuous form shown in spinner when in_progress (e.g., \"Running tests\")","type":"string"},"metadata":{"description":"Arbitrary metadata to attach to the task","type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"required":["subject","description"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"TaskGet","description":"Use this tool to retrieve a task by its ID from the task list.\n\n## When to Use This Tool\n\n- When you need the full description and context before starting work on a task\n- To understand task dependencies (what it blocks, what blocks it)\n- After being assigned a task, to get complete requirements\n\n## Output\n\nReturns full task details:\n- **subject**: Task title\n- **description**: Detailed requirements and context\n- **status**: 'pending', 'in_progress', or 'completed'\n- **blocks**: Tasks waiting on this one to complete\n- **blockedBy**: Tasks that must complete before this one can start\n\n## Tips\n\n- After fetching a task, verify its blockedBy list is empty before beginning work.\n- Use TaskList to see all tasks in summary form.\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"taskId":{"description":"The ID of the task to retrieve","type":"string"}},"required":["taskId"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"TaskList","description":"Use this tool to list all tasks in the task list.\n\n## When to Use This Tool\n\n- To see what tasks are available to work on (status: 'pending', no owner, not blocked)\n- To check overall progress on the project\n- To find tasks that are blocked and need dependencies resolved\n- After completing a task, to check for newly unblocked work or claim the next available task\n- **Prefer working on tasks in ID order** (lowest ID first) when multiple tasks are available, as earlier tasks often set up context for later ones\n\n## Output\n\nReturns a summary of each task:\n- **id**: Task identifier (use with TaskGet, TaskUpdate)\n- **subject**: Brief description of the task\n- **status**: 'pending', 'in_progress', or 'completed'\n- **owner**: Agent ID if assigned, empty if available\n- **blockedBy**: List of open task IDs that must be resolved first (tasks with blockedBy cannot be claimed until dependencies resolve)\n\nUse TaskGet with a specific task ID to view full details including description and comments.\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"TaskUpdate","description":"Use this tool to update a task in the task list.\n\n## When to Use This Tool\n\n**Mark tasks as resolved:**\n- When you have completed the work described in a task\n- When a task is no longer needed or has been superseded\n- IMPORTANT: Always mark your assigned tasks as resolved when you finish them\n- After resolving, call TaskList to find your next task\n\n- ONLY mark a task as completed when you have FULLY accomplished it\n- If you encounter errors, blockers, or cannot finish, keep the task as in_progress\n- When blocked, create a new task describing what needs to be resolved\n- Never mark a task as completed if:\n  - Tests are failing\n  - Implementation is partial\n  - You encountered unresolved errors\n  - You couldn't find necessary files or dependencies\n\n**Delete tasks:**\n- When a task is no longer relevant or was created in error\n- Setting status to `deleted` permanently removes the task\n\n**Update task details:**\n- When requirements change or become clearer\n- When establishing dependencies between tasks\n\n## Fields You Can Update\n\n- **status**: The task status (see Status Workflow below)\n- **subject**: Change the task title (imperative form, e.g., \"Run tests\")\n- **description**: Change the task description\n- **activeForm**: Present continuous form shown in spinner when in_progress (e.g., \"Running tests\")\n- **owner**: Change the task owner (agent name)\n- **metadata**: Merge metadata keys into the task (set a key to null to delete it)\n- **addBlocks**: Mark tasks that cannot start until this one completes\n- **addBlockedBy**: Mark tasks that must complete before this one can start\n\n## Status Workflow\n\nStatus progresses: `pending` ‚Üí `in_progress` ‚Üí `completed`\n\nUse `deleted` to permanently remove a task.\n\n## Staleness\n\nMake sure to read a task's latest state using `TaskGet` before updating it.\n\n## Examples\n\nMark task as in progress when starting work:\n```json\n{\"taskId\": \"1\", \"status\": \"in_progress\"}\n```\n\nMark task as completed after finishing work:\n```json\n{\"taskId\": \"1\", \"status\": \"completed\"}\n```\n\nDelete a task:\n```json\n{\"taskId\": \"1\", \"status\": \"deleted\"}\n```\n\nClaim a task by setting owner:\n```json\n{\"taskId\": \"1\", \"owner\": \"my-name\"}\n```\n\nSet up task dependencies:\n```json\n{\"taskId\": \"2\", \"addBlockedBy\": [\"1\"]}\n```\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"taskId":{"description":"The ID of the task to update","type":"string"},"subject":{"description":"New subject for the task","type":"string"},"description":{"description":"New description for the task","type":"string"},"activeForm":{"description":"Present continuous form shown in spinner when in_progress (e.g., \"Running tests\")","type":"string"},"status":{"description":"New status for the task","anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["pending","in_progress","completed"]},{"type":"string","const":"deleted"}]},"addBlocks":{"description":"Task IDs that this task blocks","type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"addBlockedBy":{"description":"Task IDs that block this task","type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"owner":{"description":"New owner for the task","type":"string"},"metadata":{"description":"Metadata keys to merge into the task. Set a key to null to delete it.","type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"required":["taskId"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"WebFetch","description":"IMPORTANT: WebFetch WILL FAIL for authenticated or private URLs. Before using this tool, check if the URL points to an authenticated service (e.g. Google Docs, Confluence, Jira, GitHub). If so, look for a specialized MCP tool that provides authenticated access.\n\n- Fetches content from a specified URL and processes it using an AI model\n- Takes a URL and a prompt as input\n- Fetches the URL content, converts HTML to markdown\n- Processes the content with the prompt using a small, fast model\n- Returns the model's response about the content\n- Use this tool when you need to retrieve and analyze web content\n\nUsage notes:\n  - IMPORTANT: If an MCP-provided web fetch tool is available, prefer using that tool instead of this one, as it may have fewer restrictions.\n  - The URL must be a fully-formed valid URL\n  - HTTP URLs will be automatically upgraded to HTTPS\n  - The prompt should describe what information you want to extract from the page\n  - This tool is read-only and does not modify any files\n  - Results may be summarized if the content is very large\n  - Includes a self-cleaning 15-minute cache for faster responses when repeatedly accessing the same URL\n  - When a URL redirects to a different host, the tool will inform you and provide the redirect URL in a special format. You should then make a new WebFetch request with the redirect URL to fetch the content.\n  - For GitHub URLs, prefer using the gh CLI via Bash instead (e.g., gh pr view, gh issue view, gh api).\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"url":{"description":"The URL to fetch content from","type":"string","format":"uri"},"prompt":{"description":"The prompt to run on the fetched content","type":"string"}},"required":["url","prompt"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"WebSearch","description":"\n- Allows Claude to search the web and use the results to inform responses\n- Provides up-to-date information for current events and recent data\n- Returns search result information formatted as search result blocks, including links as markdown hyperlinks\n- Use this tool for accessing information beyond Claude's knowledge cutoff\n- Searches are performed automatically within a single API call\n\nCRITICAL REQUIREMENT - You MUST follow this:\n  - After answering the user's question, you MUST include a \"Sources:\" section at the end of your response\n  - In the Sources section, list all relevant URLs from the search results as markdown hyperlinks: [Title](URL)\n  - This is MANDATORY - never skip including sources in your response\n  - Example format:\n\n    [Your answer here]\n\n    Sources:\n    - [Source Title 1](https://example.com/1)\n    - [Source Title 2](https://example.com/2)\n\nUsage notes:\n  - Domain filtering is supported to include or block specific websites\n  - Web search is only available in the US\n\nIMPORTANT - Use the correct year in search queries:\n  - The current month is April 2026. You MUST use this year when searching for recent information, documentation, or current events.\n  - Example: If the user asks for \"latest React docs\", search for \"React documentation\" with the current year, NOT last year\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"query":{"description":"The search query to use","type":"string","minLength":2},"allowed_domains":{"description":"Only include search results from these domains","type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"blocked_domains":{"description":"Never include search results from these domains","type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false}}],"metadata":{"user_id":"{\"device_id\":\"85fe1ca69d83f76ee3f5aedf498cd6009ee4d950524f69ea7521519e2e1a785c\",\"account_uuid\":\"\",\"session_id\":\"81396ac5-5393-4b77-89aa-7320577bcd3a\"}"},"max_tokens":32000,"temperature":1,"output_config":{"effort":"medium"},"stream":true},7:headers;992:29:6:Accept,16:application/json,]31:13:Authorization,10:[REDACTED],]36:12:Content-Type,16:application/json,]51:10:User-Agent,33:claude-cli/2.1.92 (external, cli),]68:24:X-Claude-Code-Session-Id,36:81396ac5-5393-4b77-89aa-7320577bcd3a,]28:16:X-Stainless-Arch,5:arm64,]25:16:X-Stainless-Lang,2:js,]26:14:X-Stainless-OS,5:Linux,]40:27:X-Stainless-Package-Version,6:0.80.0,]31:23:X-Stainless-Retry-Count,1:0,]30:19:X-Stainless-Runtime,4:node,]41:27:X-Stainless-Runtime-Version,7:v24.3.0,]29:19:X-Stainless-Timeout,3:600,]182:14:anthropic-beta,159:claude-code-20250219,interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14,redact-thinking-2026-02-12,context-management-2025-06-27,prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05,effort-2025-11-24,]52:41:anthropic-dangerous-direct-browser-access,4:true,]35:17:anthropic-version,10:2023-06-01,]26:9:x-api-key,10:[REDACTED],]14:5:x-app,3:cli,]28:10:Connection,10:keep-alive,]28:4:Host,17:api.anthropic.com,]46:15:Accept-Encoding,23:gzip, deflate, br, zstd,]27:14:Content-Length,6:100293,]]12:http_version;8:HTTP/1.1,}6:backup;0:~17:timestamp_created;18:1775465764.7816234^7:comment;0:;8:metadata;0:}6:marked;0:;9:is_replay;0:~11:intercepted;5:false!11:server_conn;4099:3:via;0:~19:timestamp_tcp_setup;17:1775465472.985891^7:address;27:17:api.anthropic.com;3:443#]19:timestamp_tls_setup;18:1775465473.0179305^13:timestamp_end;0:~15:timestamp_start;17:1775465472.953822^3:sni;17:api.anthropic.com;11:tls_version;7:TLSv1.3;11:cipher_list;0:]6:cipher;22:TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384;11:alpn_offers;0:]4:alpn;0:,16:certificate_list;3569:1318:-----BEGIN CERTIF